WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.578
Three months after a new intake process launched,

2
00:00:02.578 --> 00:00:04.906
a manager in the leadership meeting says frontline

3
00:00:04.906 --> 00:00:06.070
staff are resisting it.

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.140
Two other supervisors say the same thing.

5
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.850
As a director, I'd hear that as a

6
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:11.630
change management problem.

7
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.220
More training, more communication.

8
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:15.430
Maybe.

9
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.387
But resistance is often information arriving in a

10
00:00:18.387 --> 00:00:19.580
form leaders don't like.

11
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.730
What are staff seeing that we aren't?

12
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.555
The research on learning organizations says culture is

13
00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:27.130
built by systems: how we gather information, how

14
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.740
we review, how we decide.

15
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.622
Then a case manager asks for five minutes

16
00:00:31.622 --> 00:00:33.855
and says the new form is causing families

17
00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:35.250
to give up partway through.

18
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.560
And I championed that form.

19
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.017
That's the moment that decides whether the next

20
00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:40.950
person comes to you.

21
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.160
What's your first sentence?

22
00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.490
Honestly?

23
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.880
Probably a defense of why we built it.

24
00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:47.330
Most of us would.

25
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.687
Try, thank you for bringing this to me

26
00:00:49.687 --> 00:00:49.940
directly.

27
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.830
Show me where families stop.

28
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.440
Then the follow-up matters even more: tell them

29
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.840
what you did with it.

30
00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.517
Because the whole floor will know how that

31
00:00:58.517 --> 00:00:59.570
conversation went by lunch.

32
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.940
Now a blameless review of a missed deadline.

33
00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.161
Partway through, a supervisor starts to say which

34
00:01:06.161 --> 00:01:07.930
case manager made the error.

35
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.940
And the facilitator has to step in.

36
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.550
Kindly and quickly.

37
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.430
Something like, let's stay with how the process

38
00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.840
worked, and come back to individual follow-up separately.

39
00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.910
The point of the review is to find

40
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.610
what the system made easy to get wrong.

41
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.511
Which is why the facilitator shouldn't be the

42
00:01:24.511 --> 00:01:26.200
person whose decision is under review.

43
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:27.450
Last one.

44
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.900
The program manager has a draft policy ready.

45
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.210
It changes how families submit paperwork.

46
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.431
The county partner and the counties haven't seen

47
00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:34.660
it.

48
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.150
Sending it as final saves weeks.

49
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.320
It might.

50
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.995
But the people closest to families will find

51
00:01:40.995 --> 00:01:41.770
the problems eventually.

52
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.480
The only question is whether they find them

53
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.580
before launch or after.

54
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.870
Share it as a draft, say what can

55
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.310
still change, and set a date.

56
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.860
So a learning organization isn't a slogan.

57
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.692
It's a set of routines leaders design and

58
00:01:54.692 --> 00:01:54.990
protect.

59
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.978
That's what this course is for: building those

60
00:01:57.978 --> 00:01:59.440
routines in your own division.
